<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Adrian I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 772–<a href="/years/795">795 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Adrian I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Adrian I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 772 to 795. Threatened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, he appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> for help. Charlemagne crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> and absorbed the Lombard kingdom in 774, strengthening the alliance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-i.jpg" alt="Pope Adrian I" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zecca_di_roma,_denaro_di_adriano_I,_772-795,_in_argento_(bibl._apostolica_vaticana).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adrian came from a family of the Roman military aristocracy known as the domini de via Lata. His father, Theodore, died while Adrian was very young, and he was taken in by his paternal uncle Theodotus, a consul, dux and primicerius. He was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 1 February 772 until his death on 25 December 795. The popes then had to contend with repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> attempts to expand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at the expense of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. Receiving no support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, they looked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carloman-i">Carloman I</a> died in December 771 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> took his lands, Carloman's widow Gerberga and her sons fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> court at Pavia. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> asked Adrian to crown the boys Kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. The pope refused, and Desiderius invaded the Duchy of the Pentapolis, which Charlemagne's father had given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in 756.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in 773 and besieged Pavia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> surrendered in exchange for their lives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> was sent to the abbey of Corbie, and Charlemagne took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a>. The pope received additions to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a> and the Pentapolis. Adrian struck the earliest papal coin, gave Charlemagne the title Patrician of Rome and from 781 dated papal documents by Charlemagne's reign instead of the Byzantine emperor's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Adrian approved the Second Council of Nicaea of 787, which confirmed the veneration of icons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, who had received its decisions in a poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation, answered with the Capitulare contra synodum of 792, and his theologians wrote the Libri Carolini; a synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 794 refused to receive them. In 787 Adrian raised the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield">Lichfield</a> to an archdiocese at the request of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>. He restored Roman aqueducts and rebuilt the churches of Santa Maria in Cosmedin and San Marco.</p><p>His pontificate was the longest since Saint Peter until that of Pius VI in the late 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> commissioned Adrian's epitaph in 796 and held a competition for its text, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> won. Carved in Roman square capitals on black limestone, it is now in the portico of St Peter's, a notable example of Carolingian monumental script.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Adrian I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lombard kingdom was absorbed in 774, when Charlemagne crossed the Alps at <b>Pope Adrian I</b>'s request, deposed King Desiderius and took the title King of the Lombards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=783" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Adrian I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=783">What was here in 783 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
